From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 17:07:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] renderer: specialised fragment programs; a fill-rate test
 switch

The texture function, the use of the texture's alpha, an alpha-less texture, the alpha test's comparison and the blend
fix-up were uniforms, chosen per fragment by a chain of comparisons. The PS Vita's compiler turns that into fragment
programs of 110 to 190 instructions (read from the compiled GXP headers of the shipped shader cache). They are now part
of the program key and compiled in: the programs Infected uses in a mission are 5 to 25 instructions, and an alpha test
that always passes has no discard. GL_SPECIALIZE=0 (--set specialize=0) restores the uniform-driven programs.

Verified: 25 gameplay frames replayed on desktop GL are pixel-identical with and without specialisation; the shader
lint compiles all 466 programs; in Vita3K (vitaGL's translator and the real shader compiler) a bot completed mission 1
with 17 specialised programs, all linked. Not yet measured on a console.

GL_FILL_TEST=1 cuts every game draw and clear to a 2x2 scissor, to tell a fill-bound frame from one bound elsewhere.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/docs/GL_RENDERER.md b/docs/GL_RENDERER.md
index 58354be..4099fc6 100644
--- a/docs/GL_RENDERER.md
+++ b/docs/GL_RENDERER.md
@@ -44,9 +44,14 @@ setting (the names are `gl_setting_names()` in `gl_renderer.hpp`), and `<PREFIX>
 * **A draw is a vertex run plus a `DrawState`**: every GL state, uniform and texture it needs, as plain 32-bit
   words. A draw whose state is memcmp-equal to the batch in flight is appended to it; anything else flushes the
   batch. Applying a batch goes through caches that skip every GL call whose result is already in effect.
-* **Programs are keyed by six flags** (through, light, fog, texture, alpha test, rect clamp); everything else
-  a draw varies in (texture function, alpha function and reference, blend mode, lights, fog, matrices) is a
-  uniform. Eleven programs are enough for Infected from the logos to a mission.
+* **Programs are keyed by six flags** (through, light, fog, texture, alpha test, rect clamp) **and, by default, the
+  fragment stage's choices** (texture function, whether the texture's alpha is used, an alpha-less texture, the
+  alpha test's comparison, the blend fix-up below). Lights, fog, matrices and the alpha reference are uniforms.
+  The choices used to be uniforms too, which kept the programs to eleven; on the PS Vita's GPU that made every
+  fragment program 110 to 190 instructions (read from the compiled GXP headers), most of it comparisons and
+  branches the pixel never takes. Specialised, a textured, fogged, alpha-tested program is a handful of
+  instructions, and an alpha test that always passes has no `discard`. `GL_SPECIALIZE=0` (`--set specialize=0`)
+  goes back to the uniform-driven programs; both give the same pictures.
 * **Blending without `glBlendColor`.** `GL_CONSTANT_COLOR` factors are used only where the driver has them and
   the setting `no_blend_color` is off; otherwise the fragment shader pre-multiplies (`u_blend.x`: 1 doubles the
   source alpha, 2 doubles and complements, 3 multiplies by a fixed colour, 4 a fixed colour against its
@@ -141,6 +146,10 @@ transform+light+alpha           lit, no texture
 ```
 
 A game that uses more flags compiles more programs on first use; the count stays small enough to list and warm.
+With specialisation each of these splits by texture function, alpha comparison and blend fix-up: a few dozen
+programs in practice, compiled on first use and kept by vitaGL's shader cache (the VPK ships the ones a bot run
+compiled). `glsl::specialized_lint_flags()` is a sample with every choice at least once, for the shader lint
+(`ge_replay --shader-lint`) and `PREWARM_SHADERS`.
 
 ## The texture cache
 
